           (contains? names (:value (first (:operands node)))))\\\\n  3269\\\\t      (u/app (u/v 'clj/define-global!)\\\\n  3270\\\\t             (u/lit (str (:value (first (:operands node)))))\\\\n  3271\\\\t             (publish-to-globals (second (:operands node)) names bound))\\\\n  3272\\\\t      (assoc node\\\\n  3273\\\\t             :operator (publish-to-globals (:operator node) names bound)\\\\n  3274\\\\t             :operands (mapv #(publish-to-globals % names bound)\\\\n  3275\\\\t                             (:operands node))))\\\\n  3276\\\\t    :if (cond-> node\\\\n  3277\\\\t          (contains? node :test)\\\\n  3278\\\\t          (assoc :test (publish-to-globals (:test node) names bound))\\\\n  3279\\\\t          (contains? node :consequent)\\\\n  3280\\\\t          (assoc :consequent (publish-to-globals (:consequent node) names bound))\\\\n  3281\\\\t          (contains? node :alternate)\\\\n  3282\\\\t          (assoc :alternate (publish-to-globals (:alternate node) names bound)))\\\\n  3283\\\\t    (reduce (fn [n k]\\\\n  3284\\\\t              (let [v (get n k)]\\\\n  3285\\\\t                (if (or (and (map? v) (contains? v :type)) (vector? v))\\\\n  3286\\\\t                  (assoc n k (globals-child v names bound))\\\\n  3287\\\\t                  n)))\\\\n  3288\\\\t            node (keys node))))\\\\n  3289\\\\t\\\\n  3290\\\\t\\\\n  3291\\\\t(defn lower-unit\\\\n  3292\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The `clj/run-main` entry's unit for the top-level source `forms`: one\\\\n  3293\\\\t   thunk whose call runs the program — `forms` plus the definitions of\\\\n  3294\\\\t   the guest library they need, compiled unmodified under `unit-opts`\\\\n  3295\\\\t   and published to the runtime global namespace by\\\\n2026-11-10 20:15:32 GMT\\\\n2026-11-11 03:15:32 +07\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_22\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-10 20:15:32 GMT\\\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-11 03:15:32 ICT\\\\n\\\\nReviewed `8cd9fc92..778747ce` statically. All line references below are at `778747ce`. No files edited; no suites rerun.\\\\n\\\\n1. **P1 must-fix — retained tokens are source-readable and transferable.**  \\\\n   `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1546` inserts the compiler token into the map subsequently bound directly to the catch variable at `:1142`. The public escape at `:1314` authenticates only that token, without binding it to the original condition.\\\\n\\\\n   After catching a genuine condition, source can throw an unrelated map:\\\\n   ```clojure\\\\n   (throw {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/unsupported-binding\\\\n           :yang.call/data {:forged true}\\\\n           :dsg__condition (:dsg__condition e)})\\\\n   ```\\\\n   It passes authentication and escapes as a protocol condition rather than a fresh thrown payload. Reserved-symbol checks do not prohibit this keyword lookup. Token equality therefore fixes literal-`true` forgery but does **not** satisfy R5.\\\\n\\\\n   **Disposition:** bind provenance to the retained condition through an opaque association or a witness authenticating its complete contents. Add token-transplant and modified-condition regressions.\\\\n\\\\n2. **P1 must-fix — ordinary caught exceptions now expose the envelope instead of the original exception.**  \\\\n   `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1536` includes `:yang.call/thrown` in the whole-condition retention branch at `:1545`. Catch lowering still exposes that whole map (`:1142`), while `rewrite-ex-info` still implements exception accessors as direct `:yin.k/message`/`:yin.k/data` lookups (`:915`).\\\\n\\\\n   Consequently, this public-call path statically yields `[nil nil]` instead of `[\\\\\\\"boom\\\\\\\" {:x 1}]`:\\\\n   ```clojure\\\\n   (let [f (fn [] (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"boom\\\\\\\" {:x 1})))]\\\\n     (try (f)\\\\n          (catch Exception e [(ex-message e) (ex-data e)])))\\\\n   ```\\\\n   Direct intra-unit throws still expose the original payload, making ordinary catch behavior depend on routing. Whole-condition retention is necessary; changing the source-visible exception is not required by R3.\\\\n\\\\n   **Disposition:** preserve the original exception’s catching/accessor behavior while retaining the complete wire condition privately. Add a dynamic-call catch regression alongside the existing direct-throw tests.\\\\n\\\\n3. **P1 must-fix — retention can delete an original condition field.**  \\\\n   `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1546` overwrites any incoming `:dsg__condition` field. Both outbound strippers (`:1318`, `:2444`) then remove it. A genuinely transferred protocol condition containing that field as ordinary data therefore cannot be forwarded unchanged—even without source modification.\\\\n\\\\n   **Disposition:** store provenance outside the original condition, retaining the complete original map. Add a boundary fixture containing `:dsg__condition :ordinary-evidence` and assert exact preservation after immediate and saved rethrow.\\\\n\\\\n4. **P2 should-fix — two updated value-path assertions do not pin the required outer contract.**  \\\\n   `test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2620` and `:2637` inspect only the nested `:yang.call/not-callable` reason. Neither checks the outer `:yang.call/thrown` reason or callee evidence; the tail assertion also lacks an explicit error-tag check.\\\\n\\\\n   **Disposition:** assert the outer reason, error tag, inner reason, and callee evidence. The other three changed refusal expectations (`:2599`, `:2738`, `:2769`) already compare complete expected outcomes. All five wrapping changes are justified by ruling `1794332020000`; the concern is incomplete assertions, not the decision to wrap.\\\\n\\\\nThe six review questions resolve as follows:\\\\n\\\\n| Question | Assessment |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| **1. R3 retention** | **Not clean:** original-field loss in finding 3. Ordinary marker-free protocol conditions follow the intended immediate/saved rethrow path; no additional double-transfer or after-call fallthrough found in the changed bridge. |\\\\n| **2. Provenance** | **Not clean:** finding 1. Mechanically, all six writers stamp `dsg__call_token`, and both readers use token equality; no truthiness reader remains. |\\\\n| **3. Top-outcome guard** | **Clean structurally:** vector/count checks precede indexing. Descriptor maps pass through. Ordinary guest two-vectors are inside successful outcomes and are not mistaken for the outer error outcome. Provenance remains subject to finding 1. |\\\\n| **4. Ordinary behavior/tests** | **Not clean:** finding 2 and assertion gap 4. Remaining large churn is indentation, plus the documented retention, stripping, and tail changes. |\\\\n| **5. Tail semantics** | **Clean statically:** `tails/remark-tails` runs at `:2685`, after all outcome wrappers and before annotation. No new path skips re-marking where the stripper is installed. |\\\\n| **6. Scope** | **Clean:** exactly the two authorized files and two specified commits. |\\\\n\\\\nREQUEST CHANGES\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"turn.completed\\\",\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":941098,\\\"cached_input_tokens\\\":846336,\\\"cache_write_input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":4691,\\\"reasoning_output_tokens\\\":1621}}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'cell/authentic|cell/new|cell/get|cell/set|cell-seal|authentic-cell' src/cljc/yin; sed -n '1,215p' src/cljc/yin/vm/values.cljc; sed -n '1,170p' src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'cell/authentic|cell/new|cell/get|cell/set|cell-seal|authentic-cell' src/cljc/yin; sed -n '1,215p' src/cljc/yin/vm/values.cljc; sed -n '1,170p' src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2456:;; `:heap`, as a pure `vm -> vm'`. The `:cell/new` arm counts allocations in\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2773:          :cell/new\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2779:          :cell/get\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2780:          (let [id (check-ref! state :cell/get :cell-ref (:cell effect))]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2785:          :cell/set!\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2786:          (let [id (check-ref! state :cell/set! :cell-ref (:cell effect))]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2796:          :cell/authentic?\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:390:  (make-effect :cell/new {:val v}))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:395:  (make-effect :cell/get {:cell c}))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:400:  (make-effect :cell/set! {:cell c, :val v}))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:403:(defn authentic-cell?\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:410:  (make-effect :cell/authentic? {:cell c}))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:415:  {'new new-cell, 'get get-cell, 'set! set-cell!, 'authentic? authentic-cell?})\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:421:  {'new (vm/primitive-profile 'new :effectful [1] #{:cell/new} :none)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:422:   'get (vm/primitive-profile 'get :effectful [1] #{:cell/get} :none)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:423:   'set! (vm/primitive-profile 'set! :effectful [2] #{:cell/set!} :none)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:425:                                     #{:cell/authentic?} :none)})\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:121:**Purpose:** `:heap` holds the task's cells, the mutable locations `cell/new` allocates and a sealed `:cell-ref` reaches. It is VM state, not continuation state: a write is seen by every holder of the ref and survives continuation re-entry (box semantics). `:gc` is its collector's state. Both are declared record fields wherever a VM is a positional record, and both start empty in every constructor and `spawn-module` child.\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:123:**Reclamation:** deterministic, allocation-triggered, stop-the-world mark-sweep, tracing kernel positions and value positions in two modes (`ast.md` Part 6),, a pure `vm -> vm'` (`yin.vm.engine/collect`). `:since` counts allocations; when it reaches `:threshold`, the `:cell/new` effect collects before allocating, and the threshold becomes `max(:base, 2 x live)`. `:base` is a composition parameter (`:gc-threshold`, default 4096). Marking runs over the heap as it was when the cycle began, and the sweep removes only ids of that snapshot, so an id allocated at or after the cycle's counter start is spared; a budgeted marker can later reuse it. `:pinned` holds cells whose refs left the VM's view on a stream or in an FFI request; they stay live for the task's life. Ids are never reused (`:id-counter` only grows), and a ref to a swept cell is refused with `:dead-or-forged-reference`. This supersedes the cell ruling's \\\"heap reclamation can wait\\\".\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:678:Runtime-allocated mutable locations are not AST nodes. They are the `cell` host module's `cell/new`, `cell/get`, `cell/set!` and `cell/authentic?`, ordinary applications whose effects the engine interprets on all four VMs. `cell/new` answers a sealed `{:type :cell-ref :id :cell-N :seal s}`; the content lives in the task's `:heap` (see `state.md`), never in the named store. `cell/authentic?` is the ruling-of-record pure query (yang.antlr.md 8.10.12): the heap's own yes-or-no on a candidate reference, answered as data where `cell/get` would fail closed.\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:680:**Reclamation.** The heap is collected (Architect heap-reclamation design, slice 1; this supersedes the cell ruling's \\\"heap reclamation can wait\\\"). The `:cell/new` effect counts allocations, and when the count reaches the threshold it runs a deterministic, stop-the-world mark-sweep before allocating:\\n(ns yin.vm.values\\n  \\\"The in-machine closure and continuation values (D7).\\n\\n   A closure and a captured continuation are host types minted only by a\\n   kernel, never by a guest primitive or a module export. `closure?` and\\n   `continuation?` are type tests, so an ordinary map is always data,\\n   including one carrying `:type :closure` or `:type :reified-continuation`:\\n   a program cannot forge either by building its map.\\n\\n   Each type wraps the kernel's own payload map unchanged, plus an owner\\n   tag: the task the value was minted in (`owner-tag`). Lift, lower,\\n   completion and heap reclamation read the payload, so they keep operating\\n   on the maps they already understand.\\n\\n   Neither type answers keyword lookup, unlike `yin.vm.effect`'s `Effect`:\\n   a guest `get` on a closure answers nil, never its captured environment.\\n   Neither is a function: `fn?` is false, so no kernel's primitive arm is\\n   taken. Equality is structural over kind, owner and payload, and hash\\n   agrees with it, so two runs of one program give equal VM states and a\\n   closure can be a set member or a map key.\\n\\n   Both print opaquely, on every host and through `str`, `pr-str` and every\\n   guest print: `{:type :closure}` or `{:type :continuation}`, never the\\n   payload, a captured value, or the owner tag (`marker-text`).\\n\\n   These are table C laws of docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md\\n   (§4.2), which a port must meet: equal iff kind, owner and payload are\\n   equal; hash of `[kind owner payload]`; print the marker text.\\n\\n   Cell, stream and cursor references stay sealed plain data: they name\\n   table entries, are already unforgeable, and must stay usable as keys.\\\"\\n  (:require [dao.host.integer :as integer-host]\\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]))\\n\\n\\n(def marker-text\\n  \\\"The opaque printed form of each kind, shaped like the REPL's\\n   `{:type :host-fn}` marker.\\\"\\n  {:closure \\\"{:type :closure}\\\", :continuation \\\"{:type :continuation}\\\"})\\n\\n\\n(deftype Closure\\n  [owner payload]\\n  #?@(:cljd [cljd.core/IEquiv\\n             (-equiv [_ other]\\n                     (and (instance? Closure other)\\n                          (= owner (.-owner ^Closure other))\\n                          (= payload (.-payload ^Closure other))))\\n             cljd.core/IHash\\n             (-hash [_] (hash [:closure owner payload]))\\n             cljd.core/IPrint\\n             (-print [_ sink] (.write sink (:closure marker-text)))\\n             Object\\n             (toString [_] (:closure marker-text))]\\n      :clj [Object\\n            (equals [_ other]\\n                    (and (instance? Closure other)\\n                         (= owner (.-owner ^Closure other))\\n                         (= payload (.-payload ^Closure other))))\\n            (hashCode [_] (hash [:closure owner payload]))\\n            (toString [_] (:closure marker-text))\\n            clojure.lang.IHashEq\\n            (hasheq [_] (hash [:closure owner payload]))]\\n      :cljs [IEquiv\\n             (-equiv [_ other]\\n                     (and (instance? Closure other)\\n                          (= owner (.-owner ^Closure other))\\n                          (= payload (.-payload ^Closure other))))\\n             IHash\\n             (-hash [_] (hash [:closure owner payload]))\\n             IPrintWithWriter\\n             (-pr-writer [_ writer _opts]\\n                         (-write writer (:closure marker-text)))\\n             Object\\n             (toString [_] (:closure marker-text))]))\\n\\n\\n(deftype Continuation\\n  [owner payload]\\n  #?@(:cljd [cljd.core/IEquiv\\n             (-equiv [_ other]\\n                     (and (instance? Continuation other)\\n                          (= owner (.-owner ^Continuation other))\\n                          (= payload (.-payload ^Continuation other))))\\n             cljd.core/IHash\\n             (-hash [_] (hash [:continuation owner payload]))\\n             cljd.core/IPrint\\n             (-print [_ sink] (.write sink (:continuation marker-text)))\\n             Object\\n             (toString [_] (:continuation marker-text))]\\n      :clj [Object\\n            (equals [_ other]\\n                    (and (instance? Continuation other)\\n                         (= owner (.-owner ^Continuation other))\\n                         (= payload (.-payload ^Continuation other))))\\n            (hashCode [_] (hash [:continuation owner payload]))\\n            (toString [_] (:continuation marker-text))\\n            clojure.lang.IHashEq\\n            (hasheq [_] (hash [:continuation owner payload]))]\\n      :cljs [IEquiv\\n             (-equiv [_ other]\\n                     (and (instance? Continuation other)\\n                          (= owner (.-owner ^Continuation other))\\n                          (= payload (.-payload ^Continuation other))))\\n             IHash\\n             (-hash [_] (hash [:continuation owner payload]))\\n             IPrintWithWriter\\n             (-pr-writer [_ writer _opts]\\n                         (-write writer (:continuation marker-text)))\\n             Object\\n             (toString [_] (:continuation marker-text))]))\\n\\n\\n#?(:cljd nil\\n   :clj (defmethod print-method Closure\\n          [_ ^java.io.Writer w]\\n          (.write w ^String (:closure marker-text))))\\n\\n\\n#?(:cljd nil\\n   :clj (defmethod print-method Continuation\\n          [_ ^java.io.Writer w]\\n          (.write w ^String (:continuation marker-text))))\\n\\n\\n(defn owner-tag\\n  \\\"The owner tag of the task whose capability secret is `secret`: derived\\n   once, held in VM state, and carried by every closure and continuation\\n   the task mints. It reveals nothing of the secret. A task without a\\n   secret has owner nil.\\\"\\n  [secret]\\n  (when (some? secret)\\n    (jing/content-hash [:yin.k/owner secret] {:algorithm :blake3})))\\n\\n\\n(defn closure\\n  \\\"Mint a closure owned by `owner` over the kernel payload map `payload`.\\n   A trusted constructor: only kernels call it, never a guest primitive.\\\"\\n  [owner payload]\\n  (Closure. owner payload))\\n\\n\\n(defn continuation\\n  \\\"Mint a captured continuation owned by `owner` over the kernel payload\\n   map `payload`. A trusted constructor, as `closure`.\\\"\\n  [owner payload]\\n  (Continuation. owner payload))\\n\\n\\n(defn closure?\\n  \\\"True when `x` is a closure a kernel minted. A type test: no map is a\\n   closure, whatever keys it carries.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (instance? Closure x))\\n\\n\\n(defn continuation?\\n  \\\"True when `x` is a continuation a kernel captured. A type test.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (instance? Continuation x))\\n\\n\\n(defn host-typed?\\n  \\\"True when `x` is a closure or a continuation.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (or (closure? x) (continuation? x)))\\n\\n\\n(defn payload\\n  \\\"The kernel payload map of closure or continuation `x`.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (if (closure? x)\\n    (.-payload ^Closure x)\\n    (.-payload ^Continuation x)))\\n\\n\\n(defn owner\\n  \\\"The owner tag of closure or continuation `x`.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (if (closure? x)\\n    (.-owner ^Closure x)\\n    (.-owner ^Continuation x)))\\n\\n\\n(defn owned-by?\\n  \\\"True when closure or continuation `x` was minted by the task whose\\n   owner tag is `tag`.\\\"\\n  [x tag]\\n  (let [o (owner x)]\\n    (or (identical? o tag) (= o tag))))\\n\\n\\n(defn kind-of\\n  \\\"The coarse kind of `x` a refusal may carry in place of the value\\n   itself: never the value, so a refusal leaks nothing it was handed.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (cond (nil? x) :nil\\n        (boolean? x) :boolean\\n        (or (number? x) (integer-host/big-carrier? x) (cbor/float64? x))\\n        :number\\n        (string? x) :string\\n        (keyword? x) :keyword\\n        (symbol? x) :symbol\\n        (closure? x) :closure\\n        (continuation? x) :continuation\\n        (fn? x) :host-fn\\n        (map? x) :map\\n        (vector? x) :vector\\n        (set? x) :set\\n        (seq? x) :seq\\n        :else :host-object))\\n(ns yin.vm.data\\n  \\\"The `data` host module: pure collection and string primitives a\\n   language prelude is written over (Architect ruling on mutable guest\\n   objects, Q5 and Q6).\\n\\n   Every export is `:pure` with declared effects `#{}`. The module is not\\n   part of `yin.vm/primitives`: a composition whose language runtime\\n   profile names it installs it explicitly with `register-data-module`,\\n   and Yin source reaches the exports as `data/count`, `data/substring`\\n   and so on. Under explicit limits it also exports `max-items`, the\\n   composition's sequence-size limit (`register-data-module`).\\n\\n   Results are identical on CLJ, CLJS and CLJD:\\n\\n   - Strings are indexed by code point, as Python indexes them, never by\\n     the hosts' UTF-16 code units: `(data/str-length \\\\\\\"a😀\\\\\\\")` is 2 on\\n     every host. An unpaired surrogate is one code point of its own.\\n   - `str-compare` orders by code point, which UTF-16 unit order does not\\n     for supplementary characters against U+E000 to U+FFFF.\\n   - Every string call decodes its whole argument, O(n), and search is a\\n     naive O(n*m) scan: a prelude loop over a string's characters calls\\n     `str->code-points` once and walks the vector with `nth`, never\\n     `char-at` per step.\\n   - An index is any number with an integral value, so `1.0` is index 1\\n     on the JVM as it is on JS and Dart, which cannot tell it from `1`.\\n   - Nothing iterates a host map or a host set into an ordered result:\\n     host iteration order differs by host. An ordered dict is prelude\\n     code, an index map beside an order vector.\\n   - A refused call throws `ex-info` with the message `refusal-message`\\n     and ex-data built here, never host exception text: `::op`,\\n     `::reason` (`:arity`, `:wrong-type`, `:out-of-range` or\\n     `:empty-separator`), and the reason's own keys.\\n\\n   Key equality in `contains?`, `dissoc`, `disj`, `hash-set` and `into` a\\n   set is the host's `=`, which differs for `1` and `1.0` on the JVM. A\\n   runtime profile normalizes guest keys before they reach these.\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [clojure.string :as str]\\n    [dao.host.float :as float]\\n    [dao.host.integer :as integer-host]\\n    [dao.host.text :as text]\\n    [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\\n    [yin.vm :as vm]\\n    [yin.vm.module :as module]\\n    [yin.vm.values :as values])\\n  #?(:cljd (:import [\\\"dart:core\\\" BigInt])))\\n\\n\\n(def module-name\\n  \\\"The module `register-data-module` installs.\\\"\\n  'data)\\n\\n\\n(def refusal-message\\n  \\\"The message of every refusal a `data` export throws.\\\"\\n  \\\"data primitive refused\\\")\\n\\n\\n(defn- refuse!\\n  [op reason data]\\n  (throw (ex-info refusal-message (merge {::op op, ::reason reason} data))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- wrong-type!\\n  \\\"Refuse argument `arg` (0-based) of `op`, which must be `expected`.\\\"\\n  [op arg expected]\\n  (refuse! op :wrong-type {::arg arg, ::expected expected}))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Indices\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(def ^:private max-safe\\n  \\\"The largest integer every host represents exactly, 2^53 - 1.\\\"\\n  9007199254740991)\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private max-safe-big (integer-host/from-native max-safe))\\n(def ^:private min-safe-big (integer-host/from-native (- max-safe)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- integral\\n  \\\"`x` as an integer when it is a number with an integral value within\\n   +/- `max-safe`, else nil. The bound keeps an index identical on hosts\\n   whose large numbers are doubles. An exact integer in any carrier\\n   answers its canonical carrier; a host double answers its truncation\\n   when it is integral (`dao.host.float/integral?`); any other number\\n   (a JVM ratio, decimal or `float`) is nil.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (cond\\n    (integer-host/safe-native? x) x\\n    (integer-host/exact-integer? x)\\n    (let [b (integer-host/promote x)]\\n      (when (and (<= 0 (integer-host/compare-big b min-safe-big))\\n                 (<= (integer-host/compare-big b max-safe-big) 0))\\n        (integer-host/demote b)))\\n    (integer-host/host-float? x)\\n    (when (and (float/integral? x) (<= (- max-safe) x max-safe))\\n      (integer-host/trunc-native x))\\n    :else nil))\\n\\n\\n(defn- index!\\n  \\\"Argument `arg` of `op` as an integer, or a `:wrong-type` refusal.\\\"\\n  [op arg x]\\n  (let [i (integral x)]\\n    (if (nil? i)\\n      (wrong-type! op arg :integer)\\n      i)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- in-range!\\n  \\\"Refuse `i` unless `lo <= i <= hi`. `::index` is always the position\\n   the call would access: the caller's index, or for `pop` the last\\n   index, `(count v) - 1`, so an empty vector reports `::hi` -1 for\\n   both `nth` and `pop`.\\\"\\n  [op i lo hi]\\n  (when-not (<= lo i hi)\\n    (refuse! op :out-of-range {::index i, ::lo lo, ::hi hi}))\\n  i)\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Code points\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- code-points->string\\n  \\\"The string of code points `cps`, each already checked to lie in\\n   0 to 0x10FFFF.\\\"\\n  [cps]\\n  (text/units->string\\n    (mapcat (fn [cp]\\n              (if (< cp 0x10000)\\n                [cp]\\n                (let [c (- cp 0x10000)]\\n                  [(+ 0xD800 (quot c 0x400)) (+ 0xDC00 (rem c 0x400))])))\\n            cps)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- string!\\n  [op arg x]\\n  (when-not (string? x)\\n    (wrong-type! op arg :string))\\n  x)\\n\\n\\n(defn- find-code-points\\n  \\\"The first index at or after `from` where `needle` occurs in `hay`,\\n   both code point vectors, or -1.\\\"\\n  [hay needle from]\\n  (let [m (count needle)\\n        last-start (- (count hay) m)]\\n    (loop [i from]\\n      (cond (> i last-start) -1\\n            (= needle (subvec hay i (+ i m))) i\\n            :else (recur (inc i))))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Collection exports\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- data-count\\n  [coll]\\n  (if (or (nil? coll) (coll? coll))\\n    (count coll)\\n    (wrong-type! 'count 0 :collection)))\\n\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff db2434dd..c9c5533c -- src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; git diff 778747ce..c9c5533c -- test/yang/clojure_test.clj'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"git show c9c5533c:src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc | nl -ba | sed -n '2380,2780p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"git show c9c5533c:src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc | nl -ba | sed -n '2380,2780p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"git: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-EkeOFTJX' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 05:51:59.983 xcodebuild[63777:121393074]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 05:52:00.202 xcodebuild[63777:121393070] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-x8VYtQXG' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 05:52:00.995 xcodebuild[63787:121393